// Money is a kind of quantity, `Currency`, with a base unit of its own: the US
// dollar, `USD`, written `$`. Any ISO code is a unit, and so are the crypto
// tickers (`BTC`, `ETH`): about 340 in all, from currency-api. A sign goes
// before the amount, `€5.50`, or after it, `5€`; the codes go after, `5GBP`.
// `$ € £ ¥ ￥ ₹ ₩ ฿ ₽ ₺ ₪ ₱ ₴ ₫ ₦` stand for USD, EUR, GBP, JPY (both yens),
// INR, KRW, THB, RUB, TRY, ILS, PHP, UAH, VND and NGN; `A$ C$ HK$ NZ$ S$`
// for AUD, CAD, HKD, NZD and SGD. `$-1` stays the term before in a sequence, so
// a negative amount is `-$5`.
//
// A sum converts the right side to the unit of the left, as any quantity does,
// by the rates in use: `omasheet rates` says what date they are as of, and
// `omasheet rates update` fetches the latest. The rates that come with the
// program are used until then, and this file is calculated with them.
//
// Money mixes with every other unit: a price a kilogram is `USD.kg^-1` and data
// for the money is `GiB.USD^-1`. A conversion from one currency to another, with
// `.to('EUR')` or a sum, is by the rates, so this file keeps to what they do not
// change.

sheet Literals

| Name      | Value    |
| --------- | -------- |
| dollars   | $5       |
| euros     | €5.50    |
| pounds    | £100     |
| yen       | ¥1_000   |
| after     | 5€       |
| code      | 5GBP     |
| bitcoin   | 0.001BTC |
| owed      | -$5      |
| uncertain | £3.5(2)  |

sheet Conversions

| Name        | Value                    |
| ----------- | ------------------------ |
| sum         | = $5 + 3USD              |
| same money  | = $5 == 5USD             |
| per kilo    | = $12 / 2kg              |
| data a buck | = 1GiB / $5              |

// A column can be of the type `Currency`, a kind of Quantity as `Mass` is: the
// unit in brackets is the one the column shows and holds, once, in its head, and
// a cell in another currency is converted to it by the rates. A bare number is
// read in that unit. Money is shown to the places it is spent in, two for the
// dollar, the euro and the yen and more for a coin worth more, unless a number
// format says otherwise. A `$` in the format shows each amount by its sign,
// `€4.50`, and by its code where a currency has none, `3.00CHF`; `$>` puts it
// after the amount, `144¥`, and `$>_` apart by a space, `144 ¥`. This sheet
// keeps each column to its own currency, so what it shows does not move with
// the rates.
sheet Prices

Item    : String
Dollars : Currency[USD; $]
Euros   : Currency[EUR; $]
Pounds : Currency[GBP; $]
Aussie : Currency[AUD; $]
Swiss   : Currency[CHF; $]
Coin    : Currency[BTC]
Yen     : Currency[JPY; $.0f]
YenAfter : Currency[JPY; $>.0f]
EuroSpaced : Currency[EUR; $>_]

| Item | Dollars | Euros | Pounds | Aussie | Swiss   | Coin     | Yen   | YenAfter | EuroSpaced |
| ---- | ------- | ----- | ------ | ------ | ------- | -------- | ----- | -------- | ---------- |
| tea  | $5      | €4.5  | £4.5   | €4.5   | 3CHF    | 0.001BTC | ¥480  | 144¥     | €4.5       |
| mug  | 12.5    | -€2   | -£2    | -€2    | 7.25CHF | 0.25BTC  | 1_250 | 1_250    | -€2        |
